# Specification Decisions

Decisions made during creation of `loop/specification.md`. Where the developer was
asked and did not respond within the interactive window, the recommended option was
chosen and is recorded here. Any of these can be overridden by the developer; if one
is overridden, update `loop/specification.md` to match and note the change here.

## D1. Game scope: "faithful core" (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) Faithful core, (b) Minimal MVP, (c) Full arcade clone.
- **Chosen:** (a) Faithful core — single-player game with all 5 classic enemy types
  (Flipper, Tanker, Spiker, Fuzzball, Pulsar), 16 well geometries cycling with color
  changes, Superzapper, lives, arcade-style scoring, endless level progression.
- **Why:** The description says "inspired by" Tempest, not a clone. Faithful core
  captures what makes Tempest recognizable while avoiding ROM-archaeology (exact
  scoring tables, 2-player alternating, cabinet quirks) that adds effort without
  serving the stated goal.

## D2. Platform: desktop-first, keyboard + mouse (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) Desktop-first keyboard + mouse, (b) Desktop + mobile touch, (c) Keyboard only.
- **Chosen:** (a) Desktop-first. Keyboard is the primary control; mouse is supported
  as an alternative because it maps naturally to the original's spinner knob.
  Touch/mobile is out of scope for v1 (listed as a future enhancement).
- **Why:** Touch support roughly doubles input/UI design and testing surface for a
  game whose core feel is precise rotary movement.

## D3. Tech stack: TypeScript + Vite + Canvas 2D, zero runtime deps (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) TypeScript + Vite + Canvas 2D, (b) plain JS with no build step,
  (c) TypeScript + WebGL (three.js/regl).
- **Chosen:** (a). Vitest for unit tests, `tsc --strict` for type checking, zero
  runtime dependencies, static `dist/` output.
- **Why:** Tempest is line art; Canvas 2D with a manual 3D→2D projection reproduces
  the vector look without a WebGL pipeline or a runtime dependency. TypeScript fits
  the developer's stated preference for typed code.

## D4. Visual style: neon vector/wireframe (chosen without asking — definitional)

Glowing stroked outlines on black, per-16-level color cycling. A Tempest-inspired
game that doesn't look like vector graphics fails the "inspired by" test.

## D5. Audio: Web Audio API synthesized SFX, no audio assets (chosen without asking)

Sound effects are synthesized at runtime (oscillators/noise). No music (the original
had none). Mute toggle persisted in settings. Avoids asset licensing and keeps the
build dependency-free.

## D6. Persistence: localStorage (chosen without asking)

High-score table (top 10, 3-initial entry), settings (mute), and highest level
reached are stored in `localStorage`. No backend, no network calls.

## D7. Single-player only (chosen without asking)

The original's 2-player mode is alternating (not simultaneous) — it is a coin-op
artifact, not core to the game. Out of scope.

## D8. Tankers carry Flippers only in v1 (chosen without asking)

The original introduces Fuzzball-tankers and Pulsar-tankers in very late levels.
V1 tankers always release two Flippers. Variant cargo is a listed future enhancement.

## D9. Scoring is "arcade-style", not ROM-accurate (chosen without asking)

Point values are inspired by the original but defined by this spec (see the scoring
table in `loop/specification.md`). Superzapper kills award no points, to keep the
panic button from being the optimal scoring strategy.

## D10. Starting-level selection is included, simplified (chosen without asking)

The original's "SKILL STEP" starting-level choice is a signature feature and a big
part of replayability, so it stays: the player may start at any level they have
previously reached, capped at level 9 for a fresh profile's first game. No score
bonus for starting deep (the original awarded one; dropped for simplicity).

## D11. No attract-mode gameplay demo (chosen without asking)

The title screen shows the logo, high scores, and controls, but does not run an
AI-played demo game. A demo player is real work with no gameplay payoff.

## D12. Planning docs live in `loop/`, game code at repo root (chosen without asking)

`loop/` holds description, spec, guides, checklists, and decision/review logs.
The game itself (package.json, index.html, src/, tests/) lives at the repository
root so the repo is the game project.
